{"title":"Exercise 4.1","markdown":{"yaml":{"title":"Exercise 4.1"},"headingText":"Solution","containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n\n\n\nRecall that the Lebesgue-Stieltjes measure $m^*$ is defined as\n$$ m^*(E) = \\inf \\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} l(A_i): A_i \\in \\mathcal{C} \\text{ for each $i$ and } \nE \\subset \\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\infty} A_i \\right \\},\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal{C} = \\big\\{ (a,b] : a,b \\in \\mathbb R \\text{ and } a<b \\big\\}$ \nand $l((a,b]) = \\alpha(b) - \\alpha(a)$ for an increasing right-continuous function $\\alpha$. \n\nWe first show that $l = \\mu$ on $\\mathcal{C}$.\nIf $b>a \\geq 0$, since $\\mu$ is measure, \n$$ l\\big((a,b] \\big) = \\alpha(b)-\\alpha(a) = \\mu\\big((0,b]\\big)-\\mu\\big((0,a]\\big) = \\mu\\big((a,b]\\big).$$\nThe remaining cases, $b \\geq 0>a$ and $0>b>a$, are proved similarly.\n\nIt is straightforward to verify that $\\alpha$ is increasing.\nTo verify that $\\alpha$ is right-continuous, let $x_n\\downarrow x$. By the identity above,\n$$ 0\\leq \\alpha(x_n)-\\alpha(x)=\\mu\\big((x,x_n] \\big).$$\n\nSince $[x,x_1]$ is compact, \n$$\\mu((x,x_1])\\leq \\mu([x,x_1])<\\infty.$$\nSince $(x,x_n] \\downarrow \\emptyset$, by continuity from above,\n$$\\mu((x,x_n])\\longrightarrow 0.$$\nHence $\\alpha(x_n)\\to\\alpha(x)$, so $\\alpha$ is right-continuous.\n\n<br>\n\nWe will show that $\\mu = m^*$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$.\n\n1. $m^* \\big( (a,b] \\big) = \\mu \\big((a,b] \\big)$\n\n    Since $m^*((a,b]) = l((a,b])$ by Proposition 4.8, \n    $$m^*\\big((a,b]\\big) = l\\big((a,b]\\big) = \\mu\\big((a,b]\\big).$$\n\n    \n2. $m^*(E) = \\mu(E)$, where $E \\in \\mathcal{B}$ and $E$ is bounded.\n\n    For every $\\epsilon>0$, there exists a sequence of sets $\\{A_n \\}$ in $\\mathcal C$ such that\n    $$ E \\subset \\bigcup_{n=1}^{\\infty} A_n, \\quad\n    \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\mu(A_n) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} l(A_n)\\leq m^*(E) + \\epsilon.$$\n    \n    Since $\\mu$ is a measure, \n    $$\\mu(E) \\leq \\mu\\left(\\bigcup_{n=1}^{\\infty} A_n \\right) \\leq \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\mu(A_n).$$\n    \n    Hence, $\\mu(E) \\leq m^*(E) + \\epsilon$. Since $\\epsilon$ is arbitrary, we have $\\mu(E) \\leq m^*(E)$.\n    \n    For the other direction, suppose that $E \\subset (-N,N]$. Then\n    $$(-N,N] = E \\cup ((-N,N] \\setminus E).$$\n    Since $m^*$ is a measure on $\\mathcal B$, \n    $$m^*\\big((−N,N] \\big)=m^*(E)+m^*\\big((−N,N]∖E \\big).$$\n    \n    Applying the inequality above to $(−N,N]∖E$, we have\n    $$\\mu\\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big) \\leq m^*\\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big).$$\n    Since $\\mu\\big( (-N,N] \\big) = m^*\\big( (-N,N] \\big)$ by Step 1,\n    \\begin{align*}\n    m^*(E) = m^*\\big( (−N,N] \\big) - m^*\\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big) &= \\mu\\big( (-N,N] \\big) - m^*\\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big) \\\\[5pt]\n    &\\leq \\mu\\big( (-N,N] \\big) - \\mu \\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big) = \\mu(E).\n    \\end{align*}\n\n    Therefore, $m^*(E) = \\mu(E)$.\n\n 3. $m^*(E) = \\mu(E)$, where $E \\in \\mathcal{B}$\n\n    Let $I_n = (-n,n)$. Since \n    $$I_n \\cap E \\in \\mathcal{B}, \\quad I_n \\cap E \\uparrow E, \\quad\n    m^*(I_n \\cap E) = \\mu(I_n \\cap E) \\leq \\mu([-n,n])< \\infty, $$\n    we have\n    $$m^*(E) = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} m^*(E \\cap I_n) = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\mu(E \\cap I_n) = \\mu(E).$$","srcMarkdownNoYaml":"\n\n\n## Solution\n\nRecall that the Lebesgue-Stieltjes measure $m^*$ is defined as\n$$ m^*(E) = \\inf \\left\\{\\sum_{i=1}^{\\infty} l(A_i): A_i \\in \\mathcal{C} \\text{ for each $i$ and } \nE \\subset \\bigcup_{i=1}^{\\infty} A_i \\right \\},\n$$\nwhere $\\mathcal{C} = \\big\\{ (a,b] : a,b \\in \\mathbb R \\text{ and } a<b \\big\\}$ \nand $l((a,b]) = \\alpha(b) - \\alpha(a)$ for an increasing right-continuous function $\\alpha$. \n\nWe first show that $l = \\mu$ on $\\mathcal{C}$.\nIf $b>a \\geq 0$, since $\\mu$ is measure, \n$$ l\\big((a,b] \\big) = \\alpha(b)-\\alpha(a) = \\mu\\big((0,b]\\big)-\\mu\\big((0,a]\\big) = \\mu\\big((a,b]\\big).$$\nThe remaining cases, $b \\geq 0>a$ and $0>b>a$, are proved similarly.\n\nIt is straightforward to verify that $\\alpha$ is increasing.\nTo verify that $\\alpha$ is right-continuous, let $x_n\\downarrow x$. By the identity above,\n$$ 0\\leq \\alpha(x_n)-\\alpha(x)=\\mu\\big((x,x_n] \\big).$$\n\nSince $[x,x_1]$ is compact, \n$$\\mu((x,x_1])\\leq \\mu([x,x_1])<\\infty.$$\nSince $(x,x_n] \\downarrow \\emptyset$, by continuity from above,\n$$\\mu((x,x_n])\\longrightarrow 0.$$\nHence $\\alpha(x_n)\\to\\alpha(x)$, so $\\alpha$ is right-continuous.\n\n<br>\n\nWe will show that $\\mu = m^*$ on the Borel $\\sigma$-algebra of $\\mathbb R$.\n\n1. $m^* \\big( (a,b] \\big) = \\mu \\big((a,b] \\big)$\n\n    Since $m^*((a,b]) = l((a,b])$ by Proposition 4.8, \n    $$m^*\\big((a,b]\\big) = l\\big((a,b]\\big) = \\mu\\big((a,b]\\big).$$\n\n    \n2. $m^*(E) = \\mu(E)$, where $E \\in \\mathcal{B}$ and $E$ is bounded.\n\n    For every $\\epsilon>0$, there exists a sequence of sets $\\{A_n \\}$ in $\\mathcal C$ such that\n    $$ E \\subset \\bigcup_{n=1}^{\\infty} A_n, \\quad\n    \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\mu(A_n) = \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} l(A_n)\\leq m^*(E) + \\epsilon.$$\n    \n    Since $\\mu$ is a measure, \n    $$\\mu(E) \\leq \\mu\\left(\\bigcup_{n=1}^{\\infty} A_n \\right) \\leq \\sum_{n=1}^{\\infty} \\mu(A_n).$$\n    \n    Hence, $\\mu(E) \\leq m^*(E) + \\epsilon$. Since $\\epsilon$ is arbitrary, we have $\\mu(E) \\leq m^*(E)$.\n    \n    For the other direction, suppose that $E \\subset (-N,N]$. Then\n    $$(-N,N] = E \\cup ((-N,N] \\setminus E).$$\n    Since $m^*$ is a measure on $\\mathcal B$, \n    $$m^*\\big((−N,N] \\big)=m^*(E)+m^*\\big((−N,N]∖E \\big).$$\n    \n    Applying the inequality above to $(−N,N]∖E$, we have\n    $$\\mu\\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big) \\leq m^*\\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big).$$\n    Since $\\mu\\big( (-N,N] \\big) = m^*\\big( (-N,N] \\big)$ by Step 1,\n    \\begin{align*}\n    m^*(E) = m^*\\big( (−N,N] \\big) - m^*\\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big) &= \\mu\\big( (-N,N] \\big) - m^*\\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big) \\\\[5pt]\n    &\\leq \\mu\\big( (-N,N] \\big) - \\mu \\big( (−N,N]∖E \\big) = \\mu(E).\n    \\end{align*}\n\n    Therefore, $m^*(E) = \\mu(E)$.\n\n 3. $m^*(E) = \\mu(E)$, where $E \\in \\mathcal{B}$\n\n    Let $I_n = (-n,n)$. Since \n    $$I_n \\cap E \\in \\mathcal{B}, \\quad I_n \\cap E \\uparrow E, \\quad\n    m^*(I_n \\cap E) = \\mu(I_n \\cap E) \\leq \\mu([-n,n])< \\infty, $$\n    we have\n    $$m^*(E) = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} m^*(E \\cap I_n) = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\mu(E \\cap I_n) = \\mu(E).$$"},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":true,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"html","fig-align":"default","fig-pos":null,"fig-env":null,"code-fold":"none","code-overflow":"scroll","code-link":false,"code-line-numbers":false,"code-tools":false,"tbl-colwidths":"auto","merge-includes":true,"inline-includes":false,"preserve-yaml":false,"latex-auto-mk":true,"latex-auto-install":true,"latex-clean":true,"latex-min-runs":1,"latex-max-runs":10,"latex-makeindex":"makeindex","latex-makeindex-opts":[],"latex-tlmgr-opts":[],"latex-input-paths":[],"latex-output-dir":null,"link-external-icon":false,"link-external-newwindow":false,"self-contained-math":false,"format-resources":[],"notebook-links":true},"pandoc":{"standalone":true,"wrap":"none","default-image-extension":"png","to":"html","number-sections":false,"output-file":"exercise-4-1.html"},"language":{"toc-title-document":"Table of contents","toc-title-website"